<Base title="Storage and durability" description="How PostgreSQL keeps rows in 8 kB pages, why COMMIT waits for the write-ahead log, and what survives a crash, a torn page or a disk that answers too early.">
  <article class="visual-essay relational-design storage-essay">
    <StorageDefs />
    <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage</p><h1>Storage and durability</h1><div class="essay-meta"><span>PostgreSQL 18</span><span>Eleven step-through animations</span></div></header>

    <p><strong>Durability</strong> is the promise that a committed change survives a crash. Once PostgreSQL tells your application that <code>COMMIT</code> succeeded, the change will still be there after a power cut, a kernel panic or a killed server process.</p>
    <p>PostgreSQL keeps that promise without writing your table to disk at commit time. It writes a short description of the change to a log, waits until the log is on disk, and then replies. The table itself catches up later.</p>
    <p>This article follows one change through that machinery. A shop’s <code>products</code> table holds P7, a blue mug priced at 20.00, and someone raises the price to 22.00. On the way you’ll see where rows live on disk, how they reach memory, why an <code>UPDATE</code> leaves the old row behind, and what each kind of failure can take away. The examples use PostgreSQL 18 with its default settings on one server. The page numbers, transaction IDs and log positions in them are invented.</p>
    <Sql code={update} label="The price change this article follows" />

