    <section aria-labelledby="pages"><h2 id="pages">Pages</h2>
      <p>A <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/storage-page-layout.html"><strong>page</strong></a> is the unit PostgreSQL stores and reads. Every table is a file made of 8 kB pages, and every row lives inside one page. PostgreSQL never reads a single row from disk. It reads the whole page that holds it.</p>
      <p>Each page starts with a 24-byte header. After the header comes an array of <strong>line pointers</strong>, 4 bytes each, one per row. The rows fill the page from the other end, so the free space in the middle shrinks from both sides. Each row also has its own 23-byte header, padded here to 24 bytes, in front of its data.</p>
      <p>Press play to insert rows into an empty table. Then try a different row size.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="page-fill"
        label="Animation 1 · Filling pages"
        title="Rows fill a page from the end, pointers from the front"
        alt="Three 8 kB pages drawn as tall columns. As rows are inserted, line pointers grow down from the header and rows stack up from the bottom until the free space between them runs out, and the next row starts a new page. With 100-byte rows, 61 fit on a page; with 40-byte rows, 120; with 2,000-byte rows, 4."
        outline={[
          'An empty table file. Each page is 8,192 bytes and starts with a 24-byte header.',
          'Each inserted row adds a 4-byte line pointer after the header and places the row itself at the end of the free space.',
          'With 100 bytes of data, a row takes 128 bytes, so 61 rows and their pointers fit before page 0 is full.',
          'The next row starts page 1, and so on.',
          'With 40-byte rows, 120 fit on a page. With 250-byte rows, 28. With 1,000-byte rows, 7. With 2,000-byte rows, 4.',
        ]}
        caption="Drawn to scale, except the 24-byte header, which is drawn slightly thicker so it stays visible. The row sizes assume no NULL values and count any alignment padding between columns as data."
      />
      <p>A 100-byte row takes 128 bytes on the page, plus its 4-byte pointer, so 61 of them fit. At 2,000 bytes only 4 fit. A row can’t span two pages. When a row would be wider than about 2 kB, PostgreSQL compresses its long values or moves them into a separate <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/storage-toast.html"><strong>TOAST</strong></a> table, so that a page still holds at least four rows.</p>
      <p>An ordinary PostgreSQL table is a <strong>heap</strong>: each row goes wherever there is room, in no particular order. A row’s address is its <strong>ctid</strong>, a page number and an item number. Our blue mug sits at <code>(12,3)</code>, page 12, item 3:</p>
      <Sql code={ctid} label="Ask PostgreSQL where P7 is stored" />
      <p>An index on <code>product_id</code> stores that address, which is how PostgreSQL finds P7 without reading the whole table. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/indexes/">indexes article</a> covers that part. On disk, the table is a file such as <code>base/16384/16397</code> in the data directory; <code>pg_relation_filepath('products')</code> tells you which. Past 1 GB, PostgreSQL continues in <code>16397.1</code>, then <code>16397.2</code>, and so on.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="buffers"><h2 id="buffers">Shared buffers</h2>
      <p><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-resource.html#GUC-SHARED-BUFFERS"><strong>Shared buffers</strong></a> are PostgreSQL’s page cache: a fixed pool of 8 kB slots in memory, shared by every connection. PostgreSQL only works on pages that are in shared buffers. To read P7, it first needs page 12 in a slot.</p>
      <p>The default pool is 128 MB, which is 16,384 pages. For a dedicated server, the documentation suggests starting at 25% of RAM. Watch what happens as queries ask for products on two different pages.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="buffer-read"
        label="Animation 2 · Reading through shared buffers"
        title="The first read of a page pays; later reads hit"
        alt="Four empty buffer slots in memory above a row of table-file pages on disk. A query for P7 copies page 12 into a slot, a read. Queries for P3 and P7 then find page 12 already in memory, hits. A query for P40 copies page 31 into a second slot, another read."
        caption="Four slots stand in for 16,384. Real lookups also read index pages through the same buffers; this drawing counts only table pages."
      />
      <p>The first query for page 12 paid for a <strong>read</strong>. Every later query that needed page 12 got a <strong>hit</strong> and never left memory. Reading P7 also brought P3 and P9 into memory, because they share its page. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-explain.html"><code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE)</code></a> reports these counts on each plan node, in a line such as <code>Buffers: shared hit=3 read=1</code>. PostgreSQL 18 includes them whenever you use <code>ANALYZE</code>.</p>
      <p>A read doesn’t always reach the drive. PostgreSQL asks the operating system for the page, and the operating system may have it in its own cache. PostgreSQL relies on that second cache, which is why the documentation doubts that giving shared buffers more than 40% of RAM will help.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="versions"><h2 id="versions">Row versions (MVCC)</h2>
      <p>A <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/mvcc-intro.html"><strong>row version</strong></a> is one stored copy of a row. An <code>UPDATE</code> in PostgreSQL never changes a row in place. It writes a new version and marks the old one as replaced. Both stay on the page for a while, and each query decides which one it may see.</p>
      <p>Every version records two transaction IDs. <strong>xmin</strong> is the transaction that created it. <strong>xmax</strong> is the transaction that replaced or deleted it, if any. A query compares them with its <strong>snapshot</strong>, a note of which transactions had committed when it started. A report running in a <code>REPEATABLE READ</code> transaction keeps one snapshot from its first query to its last. Watch a report and a new query read P7 while transaction 812 changes the price.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="row-versions"
        label="Animation 3 · An UPDATE adds a version"
        title="Old and new versions share the page"
        alt="Page 12 shows items 1 to 3 with their xmin and xmax. Transaction 812 sets item 3's xmax to 812 and adds item 4, P7 at 22.00, with xmin 812. Before 812 commits, other readers still read item 3. After it commits, a new query reads item 4, while a report whose snapshot predates the commit keeps reading item 3."
        caption="Letters mark which version each reader uses. The page drawing shows only the fields that decide visibility; a real row header also holds a pointer from the old version to the new one."
      />
      <p>The report kept reading 20.00 after the update committed, while a new query read 22.00, and neither waited for the other. This is <strong>multiversion concurrency control</strong>, or MVCC: readers don’t block writers, and writers don’t block readers. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/isolation/">Isolation and snapshots</a> explains which snapshot each isolation level takes. The cost is that old versions pile up. We’ll come back to them in <a href="#vacuum">VACUUM</a>.</p>
      <p class="example-note">To see <code>xmin</code> and <code>ctid</code> change on a real table, try the <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=pg-read-write">read and write lesson</a> in the browser sandbox.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="dirty"><h2 id="dirty">Dirty pages</h2>
      <p>A <strong>dirty page</strong> is a page in shared buffers that has changed since it was last written to disk. After the update, page 12 in memory holds the 22.00 version. Page 12 in the table file still holds only 20.00.</p>
      <p>Memory doesn’t survive a power cut. Suppose a database replied to <code>COMMIT</code> while the only copy of the new price was still in memory. PostgreSQL doesn’t work this way, but it’s worth seeing why.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="dirty-crash"
        label="Animation 4 · A database without a log"
        title="A commit that lives only in memory"
        alt="Page 12 in shared buffers gets the 22.00 version and becomes dirty, while page 12 in the table file keeps 20.00. The database replies COMMIT. The power fails and memory is emptied. After restart, page 12 is read from the table file and P7 is 20.00 again."
        caption="A hypothetical design, shown for contrast. The amber outline and the word ‘dirty’ mark a page whose memory copy differs from the file."
      />
      <p>The application was told the change committed, and the change is gone. The obvious fix is to write page 12 to the table file before replying. That works, but it’s slow. One transaction can dirty pages scattered across the table and every index on it. Each is a separate 8 kB write to a different place on disk, and the commit would wait for all of them.</p>
      <p>It isn’t safe on its own either. If the power fails halfway through writing a page, the file holds part of each version. We’ll meet that problem in <a href="#torn-pages">Torn pages</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="wal"><h2 id="wal">Write-ahead log (WAL)</h2>
      <p>The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/wal-intro.html"><strong>write-ahead log</strong></a>, or WAL, is a log of every change PostgreSQL makes to a page. Each record describes one change precisely enough to repeat it. PostgreSQL makes the log durable at commit and lets the table files catch up later.</p>
      <p>Records go first into <strong>WAL buffers</strong> in memory, then into 16 MB segment files in the <code>pg_wal</code> directory. Each record has a <strong>log sequence number</strong>, or LSN: its byte position in the log. Each page remembers the LSN of the last record that changed it. Step through the price change again, this time with the log.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="wal-commit"
        label="Animation 5 · Committing with a write-ahead log"
        title="COMMIT waits for the log, not the table"
        alt="The update changes page 12 in memory and adds an update record to the WAL buffers. COMMIT adds a commit record. Both records are flushed to the WAL file on disk, and only then does the application receive COMMIT. Later a checkpoint writes page 12 to the table file."
        caption="The upper lane is memory and the lower lane is disk. The application hears COMMIT while the table file still holds 20.00."
      />
      <p>The commit waited for one thing: the WAL, up to and including the commit record, had to reach disk. On Linux, PostgreSQL calls <code>fdatasync</code> by default and waits for it to return. The table file still said 20.00 when the application heard <code>COMMIT</code>, and that’s fine. The log on disk holds everything needed to rebuild 22.00.</p>
      <p>That order is where the name comes from. The log is written ahead of the data, and PostgreSQL enforces it for every page it writes. Before a dirty page goes to the table file, the WAL up to that page’s LSN must already be on disk. A dirty page can even be written before its transaction commits, as long as its WAL went first.</p>
      <p>Writing the log is cheaper than writing the pages, because every record goes on the end of the same file. One flush can also serve several transactions. When many small transactions commit together, the first one to flush writes out all the WAL inserted so far, and the others find their commit records already on disk. This is <strong>group commit</strong>. The <code>commit_delay</code> setting can make the flushing transaction wait a few microseconds to gather more company. It is 0 by default.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="recovery"><h2 id="recovery">Crash recovery</h2>
      <p><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/wal-internals.html"><strong>Crash recovery</strong></a> is what PostgreSQL does when it starts after a crash: it replays the WAL to bring the table files up to date. It finds the last checkpoint in a small control file, <code>pg_control</code>, and reads the log forward from that checkpoint’s <strong>redo point</strong>.</p>
      <p>For each record, recovery compares the record’s LSN with the LSN on the page it changes. If the page is older, the change is missing, and recovery applies it. If the page already has it, recovery skips it, so no change is applied twice. Choose when the power fails, then watch recovery.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="crash-recovery"
        label="Animation 6 · Crash recovery"
        title="Recovery restores what reached the log"
        alt="The price change runs until the power fails at the chosen moment. On restart, recovery reads the WAL on disk from the last checkpoint. If the update and commit records reached disk, it reads page 12 from the table file, sees that its LSN is older, replays the update and then the commit, and P7 is 22.00. If the crash came before the flush, there is nothing to replay and P7 is 20.00."
        caption="Recovery always reads page 12 from the table file and brings it forward using the WAL on disk. Records that were still in memory are gone."
      />
      <p>Recovery brings back exactly the transactions whose commit records reached disk. If the power fails before the flush, the change is gone and the application never heard <code>COMMIT</code>. If it fails after the reply, the change survives. Both outcomes match what the application was told.</p>
      <p>The moment in between is the awkward one. When the power fails after the flush but before the reply arrives, the change has committed, yet the application only saw its connection drop. It can’t tell this apart from a failure before the flush. Retrying blindly could apply the change twice, which matters for a payment more than a price. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/conflicts-and-retries/">Handling conflicts and uncertain outcomes</a> shows how to make a retry safe.</p>
      <p>Recovery also replays records from transactions that never committed. That’s harmless. A version whose xmin never committed stays invisible, as it was before the crash. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/recovery/">WAL, checkpoints, and recovery</a> follows a real crash test through these steps on a PostgreSQL 18 server.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="checkpoints"><h2 id="checkpoints">Checkpoints</h2>
      <p>A <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/wal-configuration.html"><strong>checkpoint</strong></a> writes every dirty page to the table files, then records a redo point in the WAL. After a crash, recovery only needs the log from the latest redo point onwards. Without checkpoints, it would replay every record since the database was created.</p>
      <p>PostgreSQL starts a checkpoint every 5 minutes (<code>checkpoint_timeout</code>), or sooner if 1 GB of WAL has built up (<code>max_wal_size</code>). It spreads the page writes across 90% of the interval (<code>checkpoint_completion_target</code>), so they don’t arrive in one burst. Below, each WAL record changes one of six pages. Change how often checkpoints happen and compare the counts at the end.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="checkpoints"
        label="Animation 7 · Checkpoints"
        title="Frequent checkpoints: short replay, more writing"
        alt="Six buffered pages above a grid of WAL records. Each record dirties a page. At each checkpoint the dirty pages are written and become clean, and the redo point moves to the checkpoint. A record that is the first change to its page since a checkpoint carries a full-page image. At the end the power fails and recovery replays the records after the last redo point. Checkpointing every 5 records leaves 5 to replay but writes 30 pages and logs 35 full-page images; every 20 records leaves 20 to replay, writes 6 pages and logs 12 images."
        caption="Checkpoints here are instant and counted in records; real ones are timed and spread out. ‘Never’ is for comparison: PostgreSQL always checkpoints eventually."
      />
      <p>With a checkpoint every 5 records, a crash leaves only 5 records to replay. The checkpoints, though, write 30 pages, because a page that changes again after a checkpoint has to be written again. Every 20 records, the replay grows to 20 and the writes fall to 6.</p>
      <p>Frequent checkpoints also make the log bigger. The darker cells are records carrying a <strong>full-page image</strong>, a copy of the whole page, which PostgreSQL logs for the first change to each page after every checkpoint. Checkpointing every 5 records logs 35 of them; every 20 records, 12. <a href="#torn-pages">Torn pages</a> explains why they’re needed.</p>
      <p>The defaults suit most servers. If the server log warns that checkpoints are occurring too frequently, raise <code>max_wal_size</code>. Once a checkpoint completes, WAL from before its redo point is no longer needed for crash recovery, and PostgreSQL recycles those segment files, unless archiving or a replication slot still needs them.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="torn-pages"><h2 id="torn-pages">Torn pages</h2>
      <p>A <strong>torn page</strong> is a page that was only partly written when the power failed. PostgreSQL writes 8 kB at a time, but a drive only promises to write each sector whole. To a disk with 512-byte sectors, one page is 16 separate writes, and the power can fail between any two of them.</p>
      <p>PostgreSQL’s defence is <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-wal.html#GUC-FULL-PAGE-WRITES"><code>full_page_writes</code></a>, on by default. The first time a page changes after a checkpoint, the WAL record for that change carries a full-page image. Watch a page write fail partway, with the setting on and then off.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="torn-page"
        label="Animation 8 · A torn page"
        title="Sixteen sectors, and the power fails after six"
        alt="Page 12 is drawn as sixteen 512-byte sectors. The checkpointer starts writing it to the table file and the power fails after six sectors, leaving a mix of new and old sectors. With full_page_writes on, recovery copies the full page image from the WAL over it and the page is whole. With it off, recovery applies only the small change record and the page stays torn."
        caption="New sectors are striped; old ones are plain. Whether the drive writes sectors in order is not guaranteed; the drawing shows them in order for clarity."
      />
      <p>With the full image in the log, recovery doesn’t need the torn page at all. It copies the image over it and replays later changes on top. Without the image, a small change record can’t repair sectors that never arrived. That’s why the first change to a page after each checkpoint costs a whole page of WAL.</p>
      <p>New PostgreSQL 18 clusters also turn on <strong>data checksums</strong> by default. Each page carries a checksum, so a damaged page is reported rather than used. Turn <code>full_page_writes</code> off only if your storage guarantees whole-page writes. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/wal-reliability.html">The documentation</a> names ZFS as an example.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="fsync"><h2 id="fsync">fsync and write caches</h2>
      <p><strong>fsync</strong> is the system call that asks the operating system to put a file’s data on durable storage and report when it has. <code>fdatasync</code> does the same while skipping metadata it doesn’t need. Every guarantee so far rests on that report. When PostgreSQL’s flush returns, it believes the WAL is on disk. If that belief is wrong, a committed transaction can vanish.</p>
      <p>Two caches sit between PostgreSQL and the storage itself. The operating system’s page cache holds data that <code>write()</code> has accepted. The drive has its own write cache, and on many drives that cache is ordinary volatile memory. Follow the commit record down, first with a drive that honours flush requests and then with one that doesn’t.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="flush-path"
        label="Animation 9 · The flush path"
        title="A commit is only as durable as the drive’s answer"
        alt="Four layers from top to bottom: PostgreSQL's WAL buffers, the operating system page cache, the drive's write cache and its flash cells. The commit record moves down through them. An honest drive reports success only once the record is in flash, so it survives the power failure. A drive that ignores flushes reports success from its cache, PostgreSQL replies COMMIT, and the power failure erases the record."
        caption="The top three layers are memory and are lost in a power cut. An operating-system crash loses the top two; a PostgreSQL crash loses only the first."
      />
      <p>The second drive did nothing PostgreSQL could notice. It just answered too early. The PostgreSQL documentation warns that consumer SATA drives and many SSDs have volatile write caches, and that many SSDs ignore flush commands by default. Many drives built for servers have <strong>power-loss protection</strong>, enough stored energy to write their cache out when the power goes.</p>
      <p>To check your own hardware, PostgreSQL ships <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/pgtestfsync.html"><code>pg_test_fsync</code></a>, which times each flush method. A spinning disk at 7,200 rpm can complete at most about 120 flushes a second, one per rotation, so a result in the thousands from such a disk means a cache is answering. On macOS, set <code>wal_sync_method = fsync_writethrough</code> to make the drive empty its cache.</p>
      <p>A flush can also fail outright. On some operating systems, after a failed write-back, the kernel may report the error once and then forget the unwritten data, so a retry looks successful when it isn’t. PostgreSQL therefore treats a failed flush of a data file as fatal. It stops with a PANIC, restarts, and recovers from the WAL. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-error-handling.html#GUC-DATA-SYNC-RETRY"><code>data_sync_retry</code></a> setting controls this, and it is off by default.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="settings"><h2 id="settings">synchronous_commit, fsync = off and unlogged tables</h2>
      <p><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/wal-async-commit.html"><code>synchronous_commit</code></a> decides whether <code>COMMIT</code> waits for the WAL flush. It is on by default. With it off, PostgreSQL replies as soon as the commit record is in the WAL buffers. A background process, the <strong>WAL writer</strong>, flushes them soon after, within 600 ms at the default <code>wal_writer_delay</code> of 200 ms.</p>
      <p>You can turn it off for one transaction, for writes you could afford to lose, such as a page-view counter:</p>
      <Sql code={asyncCommit} label="Turn off synchronous commit for one transaction" />
      <p>Here is the price change with it off. Choose whether the power fails before or after the WAL writer runs.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="async-commit"
        label="Animation 10 · synchronous_commit = off"
        title="The reply comes before the flush"
        alt="The update and commit records go into the WAL buffers and the application hears COMMIT immediately. If the power fails before the WAL writer flushes the buffers, recovery finds neither record and P7 is 20.00. If the WAL writer flushed first, recovery replays both and P7 is 22.00."
        caption="The same model as animations 5 and 6, with one setting changed."
      />
      <p>Losing the last fraction of a second is the whole risk. Recovery replays commits in order, so the database is still consistent; it just ends slightly earlier than the application believes. If a later transaction depended on an earlier one, both are kept or both are lost.</p>
      <p>Two other options look similar and are not. <code>fsync = off</code> stops PostgreSQL from flushing anything, data files included, and after an operating-system crash or power cut the database can be corrupt beyond repair. Use it only for a database you can rebuild from scratch. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-createtable.html#SQL-CREATETABLE-UNLOGGED"><strong>Unlogged tables</strong></a>, created with <code>CREATE UNLOGGED TABLE</code>, skip the WAL entirely. They are faster to write, and PostgreSQL empties them after any crash, committed rows included.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="vacuum"><h2 id="vacuum">VACUUM</h2>
      <p><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/routine-vacuuming.html"><strong>VACUUM</strong></a> removes row versions that no transaction can see any more, and makes their space reusable. Every <code>UPDATE</code> leaves an old version behind, so a table whose rows change often needs it, even if its row count never changes.</p>
      <p>Some of the work happens before VACUUM arrives. When a page is nearly full, the next query or update to visit it <strong>prunes</strong> it, removing dead versions on the spot. If the new version fits on the same page and no indexed column changed, the update is a <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/storage-hot.html"><strong>HOT update</strong></a>, short for heap-only tuple, and needs no new index entry. Watch P7’s price change five times on a page with room for six versions. Run it once as it is, then again with a long report holding an old snapshot.</p>
      <StepFigure
        scene="vacuum"
        label="Animation 11 · Pruning and VACUUM"
        title="An old snapshot holds dead versions in place"
        alt="Page 12 holds P3, P9 and P7. Each price change adds a version of P7. With no long report, pruning removes the dead versions when the page fills, and P7 stays on page 12 with one index entry. With a report holding an old snapshot, nothing can be removed, the next version goes to page 13 with a second index entry, and only after the report ends does VACUUM reclaim the space."
        caption="Six versions per page keeps the drawing small; a real page holds dozens. Pruning leaves a 4-byte line pointer behind so the index entry still leads to the newest version."
      />
      <p>With no old snapshot, pruning kept P7 on page 12: the table didn’t grow, and the index didn’t change. The open report blocked all of that. Every old version might still be needed by its snapshot, so the new versions spilled onto page 13 and the index gained an entry. Once the report finished, VACUUM cleared the dead versions and the extra index entry.</p>
      <p>PostgreSQL can only remove a version once it is older than the oldest snapshot still open. One report left running for hours holds back cleanup on every table in its database. VACUUM also gives space back to the table rather than to the operating system: the file usually keeps its size, and new rows fill the gaps. <code>VACUUM FULL</code> rewrites the table to shrink it, but locks the table while it runs. Autovacuum runs VACUUM for you as dead versions accumulate. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/row-versions/">Row versions in PostgreSQL</a> goes further into visibility, freezing and autovacuum.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="backups"><h2 id="backups">Backups and point-in-time recovery</h2>
      <p>Durability protects committed changes from a crash. It doesn’t protect them from losing the disk, or from a mistake. If the drive holding the table files and <code>pg_wal</code> dies, there is nothing left to replay. If someone commits <code>DELETE FROM products</code>, crash recovery will faithfully keep the delete.</p>
      <p>Both need a copy kept somewhere else. A <strong>base backup</strong> is a copy of the data directory, and the <strong>WAL archive</strong> holds every WAL segment written since. Restore the backup, replay the archive, and stop at a moment you choose, such as just before the delete. This is <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/continuous-archiving.html"><strong>point-in-time recovery</strong></a>. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/backup-and-restore/">Backups and recovery</a> covers what to keep and how to test a restore.</p>
    </section>

